verdicts.correction:
Accurate on every load-bearing point; two corrections, one to a detail and one to severity.

DETAIL CORRECTION — "There is no retry" is wrong as written. `fetchWalletNfts` -> `alchemyGet` (frontend/src/nakamigos/api.js:66-70) wraps the call in `withRetry(..., { maxRetries: 2 })`, so a transient 429 IS retried twice with backoff before the envelope is returned. The accurate statement is: there is no USER-FACING retry and no error surfacing — once the internal backoff is exhausted the `error` field is discarded at TradesPanel.jsx:84 and the failure is indistinguishable from an empty wallet. (Reopening the modal remounts `OpenTradeAccept` and re-runs the effect, so a user who guesses to close-and-reopen does get a fresh attempt — but nothing in the UI suggests that.)

SEVERITY CORRECTION — high -> medium. The failure is fail-CLOSED, not fail-open: with zero tokens `selections` stays empty, `allPicked` is false (line 94), and the Confirm button is `disabled` (line 160). No wrong transaction is ever built or signed, no funds are at risk. The harm is a false factual sentence about the user's own wallet plus a blocked trade — real, but bounded and transient. Also note the line reference: the red-styled div opens at line 121, the sentence itself is line 122.

ACCURATE VERSION OF THE FINDING:
`OpenTradeAccept` (frontend/src/nakamigos/components/TradesPanel.jsx:61) loads the acceptor's inventory at line 83-85:
  const res = await fetchWalletNfts(wallet, c?.contract || contract, c?.metadataBase);
  out[contract] = res?.tokens || [];
} catch { out[contract] = []; }
`fetchWalletNfts` does not throw on API failure — frontend/src/nakamigos/api.js:660-663 catches and returns `{ tokens: [], totalCount: 0, error: "Could not load wallet NFTs. Please check your connection and try again." }`. Line 84 reads only `.tokens`, so the `error` string is dropped on the floor. The `catch` on line 85 is therefore near-dead code for this path; the laundering happens on line 84.

Line 114 (`const tokens = inventory[slot.contract] || []`) then feeds line 120's `tokens.length === 0`, which renders in `var(--red)` at line 122:
  "You don't hold any {c?.name} — you can't fill this slot."

That sentence is a confident factual claim about the user's wallet that a broken upstream produces identically to a genuinely empty wallet — the exact defect class this sweep is hunting.

FAILURE SCENARIO (verified reachable end to end): a user holding 4 Jungle Bay NFTs opens the Board tab and clicks "Accept this trade" (line 622-624, `setAcceptTarget(t)`) on a post requesting "any Jungle Bay". `OpenTradeAccept` mounts (line 698-706). Alchemy 429s through both internal retries. The modal tells them in error red they hold none of that collection and cannot fill the slot; Confirm stays disabled. They close the modal believing they are ineligible for a trade they can in fact settle.

CORROBORATION THAT THIS IS A KNOWN, ALREADY-SOLVED CLASS IN THIS CODEBASE: frontend/src/nakamigos/components/PortfolioTracker.jsx:161-167 handles the identical envelope explicitly, with a comment naming the prior finding —
  // fetchWalletNfts returns { tokens:[], error } on an API outage — surface
  // the retryable error state instead of a confident "0 NFTs" claim (F724).
  if (nftData.error && nftData.tokens.length === 0) { setError("Couldn't load this wallet's NFTs right now. Please try again."); ... }
TradesPanel was not swept in that pass. The fix is a three-line port of the PortfolioTracker guard.

ADJACENT INSTANCE, DELIBERATELY NOT ESCALATED: the second `fetchWalletNfts` in the same file (line 447-452) collapses an outage to `count = 0` for every collection, which zeroes `fillableCount` and `fillableTotal`. That one is benign by construction — the "N open trades you can fill" hint is gated on `fillableTotal > 0` (line 553), so an outage hides a true positive rather than asserting a false negative. Worth a one-line note in the report, not a separate finding.

Accurate statement of the finding (mechanism unchanged, severity medium):

Deals.jsx:768 renders the factual verdict "No deals found right now. All listings are priced at or above their trait floors." in a state that is indistinguishable from a total listings-read failure, because no error signal exists anywhere in the chain to distinguish them:
  - api.js:795-798 and 800-803 swallow both upstream failures into empty results;
  - api.js:773-783 (mergeListings) emits { listings, source } with no error field, and fetchListings resolves rather than throwing (834/839);
  - useListings.js:33 therefore computes listingsError === null (data?.error is dead code — nothing ever assigns it);
  - App.jsx:723-739 does not pass listingsError to Deals.
With both sources down, source additionally reports "opensea" (api.js:780-781), so even that is not a tell.

Important consequence for the fix: passing listingsError into Deals is NOT sufficient — it is null in exactly this scenario. The real repair is upstream: fetchListings must emit a health signal (e.g. { listings, source, error, sourcesFailed: [...] }) so useListings' existing data?.error branch stops being dead code, and Deals must gate line 768 on it — rendering "Listing data temporarily unavailable — cannot evaluate deals right now" instead of a market verdict. The same signal fixes Listings.jsx:868, which prints "No active listings for {collection} right now." from the identical empty-plus-"opensea"-source state.

Related, and arguably worse — recommend spinning out as its own finding: PARTIAL degradation. If OpenSea is down but the native orderbook returns a handful of orders, `listings` is non-empty, so Deals skips the empty branch entirely and renders live deal cards with concrete "% off" figures and working Buy buttons (handleBuy, Deals.jsx:674-708) computed against trait floors sampled from a rump book. The count>=3 guard at Deals.jsx:539-547 only filters single-listing traits and does not detect a missing source. Nothing in Deals.jsx tells the user a source is missing. That variant is on the money path.

